 figures of Christ, and of the pastors of his Church. — No more, as much as formerly. So many expressions must be understood. — Branch. Septuagint \"orient.\" — Reign. Zorobabel was not chief governor, (Calmet) though at the head of his tribe. (Haydock) — The kingdom of Christ was not of this world. Jo. xviii. 36. (Calmet) — He alone imparts justice to others. (Worthington)"],["haydock",6,null,"haydock","Saved: less disturbed. The world shall be redeemed. Lu. ii. 14. Eph. ii. 14. — Just. Hebrew \"Justice,\" by whom we are made just. He is the true God. Col. ii. 9. 14. No man could bear this title: and Grotius insidiously attempts to apply it to the Jews, though the Chaldean &c. clearly speak of the Messias. He must be read with great caution. (Calmet) — Septuagint \"This is his name, which the Lord will call him, Josedech. In the prophets. 9. My,\" &c. (Haydock) — Josedech means \"the Lord justice.\" (Calmet) — Sym. \"Lord, justify us.\""],["haydock",8,null,"haydock","Land. The deliverance from Egypt and Babylon were emblems of man's redemption, which was accomplished by far greater miracles."],["haydock",9,null,"haydock","Prophets of falsehood. He directs his discourse to them, and afterwards to the priests, being filled with amazement at their impending ruin."],["haydock",10,null,"haydock","Adulterers; impostors or idolaters. — Cursing of the Lord, or the abuse of his sacred name. (Calmet) — Unlike. Hebrew \"not right.\" (Haydock)"],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","Defiled. Hebrew \"hypocrites.\" My priests retain the appearance of religion, to join with impostors in deluding the people."],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","Jerusalem. These were far worse, as they pretended to be inspired by God, whereas those of Samaria publicly adhered to Baal; so that the people must have been foolish to have been deceived by them. (Calmet)"],["haydock",15,null,"haydock","Gall, or something mortally bitter. C. ix. 15. (Haydock)"],["haydock",16,null,"haydock","Own. To rely on private judgment, and not on the Church, is a mark of falsehood. (Worthington)"],["haydock",17,null,"haydock","Blaspheme. Hebrew \"despise;\" or Septuagint \"reject disdainfully the word of the Lord, you,\" &c. C. vi. 14. and viii. 11."],["haydock",18,null,"haydock","Counsel. Hebrew \"secret.\" Which of you is acquainted with his designs? I will inform you, that your perdition is at hand. v. 20. (Calmet)"],["haydock",21,null,"haydock","Not send. The want of mission proves a prophet or preacher to be false. (Worthington)"],["haydock",22,null,"jerome","I should. Some copies read avertissent, \"they would have turned,\" conformably with the Hebrew &c. and we should suspect that this was a mistake of the transcriber, if S. Jerom did not agree with the Vulg. in his commentary, (Calmet) though not in express terms: (Haydock) \"I also should not have abandoned them to impurity...Let us behold how heretics, having once given way to despair,...seek the gratification of their sensual appetite.\" (St. Jerome)"],["haydock",23,null,"haydock","Off? Can you so easily penetrate my designs, or escape my fury? Am I like your idols of yesterday? Septuagint and Theodot. \"I am a God at hand,\" &c. which agrees with the sequel. (Calmet)"],["haydock",27,null,"haydock","Dreams, as if God had favoured them with his revelations. (Haydock) — Fathers; the false prophets of Baal, whom these imitate."],["haydock",28,null,"haydock","Dream, to those who are really sent by God, (Calmet) and recognized. 1 Cor. xiv. 29. (Haydock)"],["haydock",29,null,"haydock","Pieces? True prophets will have a zeal fo the conversion of souls. C. xx. 9. Heb. iv. 12."],["haydock",30,null,"haydock","Neighbour, causing those whom I have sent to be disregarded."],["haydock",31,null,"haydock","Use. Hebrew also, \"sharpen, or render smooth,\" and insinuating. Ps. xlix. 16. (Calmet)"],["haydock",32,null,"haydock","Wonders. Protestants \"lightness.\" Septuagint \"frauds,\" or pretended miracles. (Haydock) — False prophets cannot work true miracles: but it is more difficult to distinguish these than the former mark of being lawfully sent. v. 21. (Worthington)"],["haydock",33,null,"haydock","Burden, Massa, (Haydock) denotes a weight, or sometimes a prediction. (Calmet)"],["haydock",34,null,"challoner","Burden of the Lord. This expression is here rejected and disallowed, at least for those times: because it was then used in mockery and contempt by the false prophets, and unbelieving people, who ridiculed the repeated threats of Jeremias under the name of his burdens. (Challoner)"],["haydock",35,null,"haydock","Answered. I will make you alter your language by chastisements."],["haydock",39,null,"haydock","Take. Hebrew of the Masorets, (Calmet) \"I, even I, will utterly forget (Protestants; Haydock) or abandon you.\" Chaldean But the sense of the Septuagint and Vulg. seems preferable. (Calmet)"]],"au":{"challoner":["Richard Challoner",0,0,1781,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/03566a.htm"],"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"],"theodoret":["Theodoret of Cyrus",0,1,457,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/14570a.htm"],"jerome":["Jerome",1,1,420,"https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/#jerome"]},"fa":[[5,null,0,"The Proof of the Gospel","Book 7",0,339,"… Therefore the prophecy in the present passage is that God will raise up \"a righteous rising\" to David, in the sense of a sun of righteousness. And he calls the same Being an understanding king, and one who does judgment and justice on the earth. He gives him too the same name as David, who died very long before. For you must note carefully how at the beginning he says, \"And I will raise up to David a righteous rising,\" and adds at the end, \"And I will raise up David to be his king. Whose, but David's?—for it was to him that he said He would raise up a righteous rising. …"],[6,null,0,"The Proof of the Gospel","Book 7",0,339,"… So it is said, \"And this is the name by which the Lord shall call them, Josedekeim among the prophets. So, then, we see that the people that are to become through the subject of the prophecy the spiritual Jews and the true Israel, will be called Josedekeim from Joshua, and they will be called by this name, he says, not by men, but by God, and by His prophets. For you must note carefully the passage that says, \"And this is the name which the Lord shall call them, Josedekeim by his prophets. And its translation in Greek is. as I said, \"God's just ones.\""],[7,8,1,"Irenaeus Against Heresies Book 5","AGAINST HERESIES 5:34.1",1,202,"Now I have shown a short time ago that the church is the seed of Abraham; and for this reason, that we may know that He who in the New Testament \"raises up from the stones children unto Abraham,\" is He who will gather, according to the Old Testament, those that shall be saved from all the nations, Jeremiah says: \"Behold, the days come, saith the Lord, that they shall no more say, The Lord liveth, who led the children of Israel from the north, and from every region whither they had been driven; He will restore them to their own land which He gave to their fathers.\""],[12,null,2,"Epistle to the Philadelphians","Epistle of Ignatius to the Philadelphians",1,108,"Wherefore, as children of light and truth, avoid the dividing of your unity, and the wicked doctrine of the heretics, from whom \"a defiling influence has gone forth into all the earth.\" But where the shepherd is, there do ye as sheep follow. For there are many wolves in sheep's clothing, who, by means of a pernicious pleasure, carry captive those that are running towards God; but in your unity they shall have no place."],[22,null,3,"To the Bishops of Africa","LETTER TO THE BISHOPS OF AFRICA 4",2,373,"This enables us to see, brethren, that they of Nicaea breathe the spirit of Scripture, in that God says in Exodus, `I am that I am,' and through Jeremiah, `Who is in His substance and hath seen His word;' and just below, `if they had stood in My subsistence and heard My words:' now subsistence is essence, and means nothing else but very being, which Jeremiah calls existence, in the words, `and they heard not the voice of existence.' For subsistence, and essence, is existence: for it is, or in other words exists."],[23,null,4,"Homilies on Matthew","Homily 54",3,407,"For were He distant from us in place, thou mightest well doubt, but if He is present everywhere, to him that strives and is in earnest He is near. Wherefore also the prophet said, \"I will fear no evil, for Thou art with me;\" and God Himself again, \"I am a God nigh at hand, and not a God afar off. Then as our sins separate us from Him, so do our righteousnesses draw us nigh unto Him. \"For while thou art yet speaking,\" it is said, \"I will say, Here I am. What father would ever be thus obedient to his offspring? What mother is there, so ready, and continually standing, if haply her children call her? …"],[23,24,5,"Exhortation to the Heathen",null,4,215,"Jeremiah the prophet, gifted with consummate wisdom, or rather the Holy Spirit in Jeremiah, exhibits God. \"Am I a God at hand,\" he says, \"and not a God afar off? Shall a man do ought in secret, and I not see him? Do I not fill heaven and earth? Saith the Lord.\" For \"the Lord who created the earth by His power,\" as Jeremiah says, \"has raised up the world by His wisdom;\" for wisdom, which is His word, raises us up to the truth, who have fallen prostrate before idols, and is itself the first resurrection from our fall."],[24,null,6,"On the Holy Spirit","Book 1",5,397,"… This cannot be said of an Angel. Lastly, Gabriel himself, when sent to Mary, said: \"Hail, full of grace,\" plainly declaring the grace of the Spirit which was in her, because the Holy Spirit had come upon her, and she was about to have her womb full of grace with the heavenly Word. For it is of the Lord to fill all things, Who says: \"I fill heaven and earth. If, then, it is the Lord Who fills heaven and earth, Who can judge the Holy Spirit to be without a share in the dominion and divine power, seeing that He has filled the world, and what is beyond the whole world, filled Jesus the Redeemer of the whole world? …"],[24,null,6,"Exposition of the Christian Faith","Book 5",5,397,"… Elsewhere also it stands that God said: \"Go to, let us go down and confound their language. God, indeed, never descends from any place; for He says: \"I fill heaven and earth. But He seems to descend when the Word of God enters our hearts, as the prophet has said: \"Prepare ye the way of the Lord, make His paths straight. We are to do this, so that, as He Himself promised, He may come together with the Father and make His abode with us. It is clear, then, how He comes."],[24,null,7,"Expositions on the Psalms","Psalm 50",6,430,"… The fairness of the field, the abundance of all things engendering upon earth, \"is with Me,\" He saith. How with Him? Were they so, even before they were made? Yea, for with Him were all things to come, and with Him are all things by-gone: things to come in such sort, that there be not withdrawn from Him all things by-gone. With Him are all things by a certain cognition of the ineffable wisdom of God residing in the Word, and the Word Himself is all things. Is not the beauty of the field in a manner with Him, inasmuch as He is everywhere, and Himself hath said, \"Heaven and earth I fill\"? …"],[24,null,8,"Treatise 4. On the Lord's Prayer","Treatise IV. On the Lord's Prayer 4",7,258,"… For as it is characteristic of a shameless person to be noisy with his cries, so it is fitting for the modest to pray with moderated petitions. Moreover, in his teaching the Lord has bidden us to pray in secret—in hidden and remote places, in our very bedchambers—which is best suited to faith, that we may know that God is everywhere present, hears and sees all and in the plenitude of his majesty penetrates even into hidden and secret places, as it is written, \"I am a God at hand, and not a God afar off. If a person shall hide himself in secret places, shall I not then see him? Do I not fill heaven and earth?\""]],"fau":[["eusebius","Eusebius of Caesarea",0,"ecclesiastical-writer",null,1,339],["irenaeus","Irenaeus of Lyons",1,"father",null,0,202],["ignatius-of-antioch","Ignatius of Antioch",0,"father",null,0],["athanasius","Athanasius of Alexandria",1,"father",null,1,373],["chrysostom","John Chrysostom",1,"father",null,1,407],["clement-alexandria","Clement of Alexandria",0,"ecclesiastical-writer",null,1,215],["ambrose","Ambrose of Milan",1,"father",null,1,397],["augustine","Augustine of Hippo",1,"father",null,1,430],["cyprian","Cyprian of Carthage",0,"father",null,0,258]],"ftr":[["W. J. Ferrar, The Proof of the Gospel (SPCK, 1920)"],["Ante-Nicene Fathers, vol. 1 (1885)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/anf01"],["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 2, vol. 4 (1892)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf204"],["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 1, vol. 10 (1888)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf110"],["Ante-Nicene Fathers, vol. 2 (1885)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/anf02"],["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 2, vol. 10 (1896)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf210"],["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 1, vol. 8 (1888)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf108"],["Ante-Nicene Fathers, vol. 5 (1886)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/anf05"]]}